# Product Requirements Document — Google Drive Sync

**Date:** 2026-07-27
**Product:** PhotoViewer (photo & video gallery app)
**Status:** Approved for planning

---

## 1. Overview

PhotoViewer can already send photos and videos to a single Google Drive account. This
feature turns that one-way, one-account backup into a proper Drive integration: the user
can connect **more than one** Google account (personal and work), choose which one they're
backing up to, see **how much room is left** in each, browse **what's already safely on
Drive**, and **pull items back down** onto the phone when they're missing locally.

The goal is that a user can answer three questions in a couple of taps: *is my library
backed up, where did it go, and can I get it back?*

## 2. The problem

- The app supports exactly one Google account. Users who keep personal and work Drives
  separate have to sign out and back in to change destination, which loses context.
- Backup is one-way. Once a photo is deleted from the phone or the user moves to a new
  device, the app offers no way to bring it back — even though the file is sitting in the
  user's own Drive.
- Nothing in the app shows how much Drive storage is left. A backup can run for several
  minutes and then fail because the account was full, with the wasted time and data
  already spent.
- Users can't see what's actually on Drive from inside the app, so "am I backed up?" is a
  question they have to leave the app to answer.

## 3. Who it's for

Existing gallery users who already keep their photos on Google Drive — particularly those
with a separate work account, those moving to a new phone, and those who clear photos off
their device to save space and later want them back.

## 4. What it does

- **Connect and disconnect multiple Google accounts**, kept in a list the user can manage.
- **Choose which connected account** is currently in use.
- **Back up** selected photos and videos to that account's backup folder.
- **See what's on Drive** for that account, and **download items back** to the device.
- **Show storage information** for each connected account — total, used, free, and how
  much of it this app's backups take.

## 5. What "sync" means here — an important limit

The app uses Google's **per-file permission** (`drive.file`), which means it can only ever
see the files **it created itself**. It cannot read the rest of the user's Drive.

Two consequences the whole design follows from:

- **Everything in this version works with the app's own backup folder.** "See what's on
  Drive" means the backup folder, not the user's whole Drive.
- **Importing photos from a folder the user made themselves** — one they filled from a
  laptop, say — is **not possible in this version**. It requires a broader Google
  permission that only unlocks after passing a third-party security assessment (an annual
  cost with several weeks of lead time). That capability is planned as **Phase 2** and is
  described in section 11.

This limit is deliberate: it keeps the feature shippable now, and it means the app can
never touch anything the user didn't hand it.

## 6. How it works for the user

### 6.1 The accounts list

A **Google Drive** entry in the gallery menu opens a screen listing every connected
account. Each row shows the account's picture and email, its **storage** (used of total,
and how much of that is this app's backup folder), how many items have been backed up to
it, and whether it's the one currently in use.

From here the user can **Add account**, **switch** to another connected account, or
**disconnect** one.

### 6.2 One account in use at a time

Accounts stay in the list once connected, but only **one is in use at any moment**.
Switching is a single tap. Most of the time the change is instant; occasionally Google
shows a brief sign-in prompt, so the interface should not promise that switching is always
silent.

If an account can no longer be used — the password changed, or access was withdrawn from
the user's Google account settings — it **stays in the list** showing a **Reconnect**
prompt, with its backup history intact. Removing it silently would look like data loss
even though nothing was lost.

### 6.3 The backup folder

Each account gets one folder created by the app. On connecting an account, the app looks
for an existing folder named **GoThru** — the name used by earlier versions — and adopts it
if found, so existing backups stay visible and nothing moves. If there isn't one, it
creates a folder named **PhotoViewer**.

There is no folder picker in this version, for the reason given in section 5: the app can
only see folders it made itself.

### 6.4 Storage information

Storage figures are refreshed when an account is connected and whenever the accounts
screen is opened.

They also do real work: **before a backup starts, the app compares the size of what's
selected against the free space in the account** and warns the user up front if it won't
fit — instead of failing partway through a multi-gigabyte upload.

### 6.5 Backing up

The backup view names the account it's sending to. The user selects everything or picks
individual items, and watches live progress. **Stop** finishes the uploads already in
flight and can be resumed later. Anything already in that account's folder is skipped
automatically, so re-running a backup only sends what's new.

**"Backed up" is per account.** A photo saved to the personal Drive shows as *not* backed
up while the work account is in use. This is correct behaviour, but it will read as a bug
unless the interface is explicit — so badges, counts and empty states must always name the
account they refer to.

### 6.6 Seeing what's on Drive, and getting it back

An **On Drive** view lists the current account's backup folder, grouped by date, newest
first, with a thumbnail, name and size for each item.

Every item is marked **On this device** or **Not on this device**. Items already on the
phone are not selectable by default, so the ordinary path can't create duplicates.

The user selects items that are missing and taps **Download**. They're saved into the
device's gallery — with progress and a **Stop** button, as with backup — and then appear in
the app's library.

### 6.7 Disconnecting

Disconnecting an account withdraws the app's access and removes the account from the list.

- **Files on Drive are never touched.** They belong to the user.
- **The record of what was backed up there is kept**, so reconnecting the same account
  restores the full picture immediately rather than re-reading everything.

## 7. Safety principles

- **The app can only see what it put there.** By design it has no access to the rest of the
  user's Drive.
- **Nothing is deleted, anywhere, by this feature.** Deletions do not travel between the
  phone and Drive in either direction.
- **Downloads can't quietly duplicate a library.** Items already on the device are marked
  and excluded from selection by default.
- **No silent surprises about which account is in use.** Every screen that shows backup
  state names the account it belongs to.
- **Fail before spending the user's data, not during.** The free-space check runs before a
  backup starts.

## 8. Success metrics

**Adoption** — share of users who connect at least one account, and the **distribution of
accounts per user**. The second number is the honest test of whether multi-account earned
its complexity; if almost everyone connects exactly one, that's a finding.

**Backup** — sessions started versus completed, total volume uploaded, and average session
size.

**Restore** — share of users who ever download something back, and how much.

**Health** — re-authorization failure rate, reconnect rate, disconnect rate, and how often
the "won't fit in free space" warning fires.

## 9. Out of scope for this version

- Browsing or importing folders the user created themselves (see Phase 2, section 11).
- Automatic or background backup — backup is started by the user, every time.
- Deletion travelling between the device and Drive, in either direction.
- Shared drives and Team Drives.
- Backing up to several accounts at once, or moving files between connected accounts.
- Renaming, editing or organising Drive files from inside the app.
- **Google Photos.** Users conflate the two, so this is worth stating plainly: this feature
  works with Google Drive only and does not read or write a user's Google Photos library.
  That is a separate service with its own permissions.

## 10. Assumptions & notes

- Uploads use each user's own Drive storage quota. The developer is never billed for it.
- Backups are matched to their original photo by a tag the app writes on each uploaded
  file, so "already backed up?" is an exact lookup rather than a guess from name or size.
- The same tag decides **On this device**: an item counts as present only when the phone
  still holds the original it was uploaded from. A file that reaches Drive by some other
  route, or one whose local original is gone, shows as **Not on this device** and can be
  downloaded. The app does not compare file names or contents to guess at matches.
- Existing users who already backed up to the old **GoThru** folder keep those backups and
  see them in the On Drive view; nothing is renamed or moved.
- On phones that grant access only to selected photos, backup covers what the app can see,
  as elsewhere in the app.
- Switching accounts may occasionally require a Google sign-in prompt. This is a platform
  behaviour, not something the app can guarantee away.

## 11. Phase 2 — importing from the user's own Drive folders

The most-requested version of "sync a Drive folder" is choosing any folder in Drive and
bringing its photos and videos into the gallery. This needs a broader Google permission
than the app uses today, which in turn requires:

- Google OAuth verification, plus a **third-party security assessment** — a recurring
  annual cost with several weeks of lead time.
- The feature cannot be released until that assessment passes.

It is therefore deliberately separated from this version, so multi-account backup, restore
and storage information can ship without waiting on an external review. If Phase 2 is
approved, the assessment should be started early, since its lead time — not the
development work — is the long pole.
